name chess-openings
match a.tsv b.tsv c.tsv d.tsv e.tsv
tier opening by \n
header
tier field by \t
# An opening is an incomplete game: its pgn is movetext with no tag pairs and no result, read by chess (src/chess.c,
# the native grammar pgn) as the line a game of the same moves plays, [P0, M0, P1, ..., Pn], replayed from the standard
# start position. The line is the row's content, the route the book gives, so it shares its positions and moves with
# every game that plays it.
part pgn grammar pgn
content eco name pgn
# An opening names a position, a fixed board state: the Queen's Gambit is the position after 1. d4 d5 2. c4, and its
# Accepted and Declined are later ones. So what the row says is said of the position its line ends in (chess's product
# position of the pgn), and a game that transposes into that position reaches the same name.
thing opening pgn product position
# ECO is a classification, not an identity or a game result. No win/loss evidence is invented.
relation "opening name" name
relation "Encyclopaedia of Chess Openings code" eco
attest opening name eco
